<Page path="concepts/networking">
<section id="problem">
  <h2 id="problem"><a href="#problem">The problem it solves</a></h2>
  <p>Two copies of the same stack cannot both publish port 8000. The usual answer is to give the second copy other ports, then teach every front-end, callback URL and developer about them. multiversectl allocates no ports: every universe is reached through <strong>one shared proxy</strong> on <strong>one set of ports</strong>, and requests are told apart by the <code>Host</code> header. The tenth universe costs no ports and changes no configuration anywhere.</p>
</section>

<section id="proxy">
  <h2 id="proxy"><a href="#proxy">The shared proxy</a></h2>
  <p>One Traefik container for the machine, in its own compose project <code>multiverse-proxy</code>. The first <code>up</code> starts it. It discovers routes from the container runtime, so starting a universe is all the configuration it needs; multiversectl writes the labels into a generated fragment, one per app, which is why your own compose files say nothing about Traefik:</p>
  <Terminal kind="file"><pre><span class="c"># universes/3/.multiverse-routes-api.yml, rewritten on every up</span>
<span class="k">services</span>:
  <span class="k">web</span>:
    <span class="k">ports</span>: !reset []
    <span class="k">labels</span>:
      <span class="k">traefik.enable</span>: <span class="s">"true"</span>
      <span class="k">traefik.docker.network</span>: <span class="s">"acme-u3-api_default"</span>
      <span class="k">traefik.http.services.api-u3.loadbalancer.server.port</span>: <span class="s">"8001"</span>
      <span class="k">traefik.http.routers.api-u3.rule</span>: <span class="s">"Host(`api-u3.example.test`)"</span>
      <span class="k">traefik.http.routers.api-u3.entrypoints</span>: <span class="s">"web"</span></pre></Terminal>
  <p>The proxy reaches a service on <strong>the app's own network</strong> &mdash; the network compose puts it on, read from the resolved configuration, which is the project's <code>default</code> network (<code>acme-u3-api_default</code>) unless your compose files name one themselves &mdash; and joins it once the app is up: a connection made to the running proxy, which is neither restarted nor recreated when a universe comes or goes. It leaves the network before the app is taken down. Services are not attached to any network shared with other universes, on purpose: on a network every service answers to its plain name, so <code>auth</code> there would be the <code>auth</code> of every universe at once, and a service calling <code>http://auth:8080</code> would reach a random universe's. A host reached by IP alone (<code>portForward: only</code>, <code>domain: none</code>) has no route in the proxy and is on no proxy network at all.</p>
  <p>The proxy's own compose file still names one network, <code>multiversenet</code> by default, so older universes keep answering until they are brought up again; <code>universe doctor</code> lists the containers still on it. A proxy that is created again joins the networks of the containers that name it in their <code>io.multiversectl.served-by</code> label, and it leaves every network of an app's compose project before that app is taken down.</p>
  <p>Each network takes a subnet from Docker's address pools, which bound how many networks the machine can have. <code>doctor</code> reports how many are left, because the failure &mdash; <em>all predefined address pools have been fully subnetted</em> &mdash; says nothing about what to change.</p>
  <p class="muted">Edit the manifest or your compose files, never the generated one: it is replaced on every start.</p>
</section>

<section id="entrypoints">
  <h2 id="entrypoints"><a href="#entrypoints">Entrypoints</a></h2>
  <p>An entrypoint is a named port the proxy listens on, shared by every universe.</p>
  <Terminal kind="file"><pre><span class="k">spec</span>:
  <span class="k">proxy</span>:
    <span class="k">entrypoints</span>:
      - <span class="k">name</span>: web
        <span class="k">port</span>: 80
      - <span class="k">name</span>: devapi
        <span class="k">port</span>: 8001</pre></Terminal>
  <p>One is usually enough. The second is for a browser application built to call its API on a fixed port: point the proxy at that port too and the API answers there under every universe's own hostname, so the hard-coded <code>:8001</code> keeps working everywhere, unchanged. A host rule picks its entrypoint by name, defaulting to <code>web</code>.</p>
</section>

<section id="hostnames">
  <h2 id="hostnames"><a href="#hostnames">Hostnames</a></h2>
  <p>Every address is <code>&lt;host-key&gt;-&lt;slug&gt;.&lt;domain&gt;</code> — the host key names the address, the slug names the universe:</p>
  <Terminal kind="output"><pre>api-u3.example.test         <span class="c"># host api, universe 3</span>
adm-u3.example.test         <span class="c"># host adm, universe 3</span>
api-fix-login.example.test  <span class="c"># host api, universe fix/login</span></pre></Terminal>
  <p>Flat rather than nested, on purpose: <code>api-u3.example.test</code> is one label under the domain, so a single wildcard certificate for <code>*.example.test</code> covers every address of every universe. <code>api.u3.example.test</code> would need one per universe, because a wildcard matches one label.</p>
  <p>A service can claim several hostnames, which is how one API answers under each front-end's own name — see <a href="/docs/concepts/labels/">Labels</a>.</p>
</section>

<section id="domains">
  <h2 id="domains"><a href="#domains">Domains</a></h2>
  <p><code>spec.domain</code> is the wildcard domain hostnames are built under. The default, <code>auto</code>, derives one from how the multiverse is exposed.</p>
  <div class="tw"><table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Setting</th><th scope="col">Hostnames</th><th scope="col">Resolution</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr><td data-l="Setting"><code>auto</code> with <code>expose: lan</code></td><td data-l="Hostnames"><code>api-u3.192-168-1-20.traefik.me</code></td><td data-l="Resolution">A public wildcard DNS service answers with the address in the name, so any device on the network resolves it with nothing installed.</td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Setting"><code>auto</code> with <code>expose: local</code></td><td data-l="Hostnames"><code>api-u3.localhost</code></td><td data-l="Resolution">Browsers resolve <code>*.localhost</code> themselves. No DNS query leaves the machine.</td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Setting"><code>domain: none</code></td><td data-l="Hostnames">none: <code>http://192.168.1.20:41000</code></td><td data-l="Resolution">Nothing is looked up, by anyone. Every host is forwarded to a port of its own on the machine's address, so the proxy is out of the path; <code>expose</code> still decides the interface.</td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Setting">A domain you own</td><td data-l="Hostnames"><code>api-u3.dev.example.com</code></td><td data-l="Resolution">Yours to point at the machine, with your own wildcard certificate (below).</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>
  <h3 id="tls">Serving https</h3>
  <p>Give an entrypoint <code>tls: true</code> and the proxy serves every host on it over https. Every address handed to a service or shown (<code>&lt;NAME&gt;_URL</code>, <code>MULTIVERSE_ORIGINS</code>, <code>universe ls</code>) then says <code>https://</code>, so a framework that checks the <code>Origin</code> header sees the one the browser sends. With no certificate the proxy presents its own self-signed one; for a domain you own, name a wildcard certificate for <code>*.&lt;domain&gt;</code> and it covers every universe:</p>
  <Terminal kind="file"><pre><span class="k">spec</span>:
  <span class="k">domain</span>: dev.example.com
  <span class="k">proxy</span>:
    <span class="k">entrypoints</span>:
      - &#123;<span class="k">name</span>: web, <span class="k">port</span>: 443, <span class="k">tls</span>: true&#125;
    <span class="k">certificates</span>:
      - &#123;<span class="k">certFile</span>: ~/certs/wildcard.pem, <span class="k">keyFile</span>: ~/certs/wildcard-key.pem&#125;</pre></Terminal>
  <p>The certificates are files on the machine, mounted into the proxy read-only; a relative path is relative to the manifest. A renewed certificate is read when the proxy next starts. A forwarded address (<code>portForward</code>) reaches the service without the proxy and stays <code>http://</code>.</p>
</section>

<section id="no-dns">
  <h2 id="no-dns"><a href="#no-dns">Without any DNS name</a></h2>
  <p>The default <code>lan</code> setting builds names under a public wildcard service, which puts your machine's address in a stranger's DNS logs and needs a resolver that answers it. <code>domain: none</code> opts out entirely:</p>
  <Terminal kind="file"><pre><span class="k">spec</span>:
  <span class="k">expose</span>: lan       <span class="c"># 0.0.0.0, so another device can reach it</span>
  <span class="k">domain</span>: none      <span class="c"># and no name anywhere</span></pre></Terminal>
  <p>Each host is published on a free port of its own on the machine's address (the <a href="/docs/concepts/labels/#host-forward">portForward</a> mechanism, applied to every host), and that <code>http://&lt;ip&gt;:&lt;port&gt;</code> is what <code>ls</code>, the web view, <code>&lt;HOST&gt;_URL</code> and <code>origins</code> show. The port is chosen once and kept for the life of the universe. With <code>expose: local</code> it is <code>127.0.0.1</code>. The cost is the one a name was hiding: a front-end built to call its API on a fixed port (an entrypoint with <code>alsoMatch</code>) cannot be given that port by every universe, so such an app needs the address from <code>&lt;HOST&gt;_URL</code> instead.</p>
</section>

<section id="expose">
  <h2 id="expose"><a href="#expose">Who can reach a universe</a></h2>
  <Terminal kind="file"><pre><span class="k">spec</span>:
  <span class="k">expose</span>: lan     <span class="c"># default</span>
  <span class="c"># expose: local</span></pre></Terminal>
  <p><code>lan</code> publishes the proxy's ports on every interface and derives a publicly resolvable domain, so a phone or a colleague on the same network opens a universe with nothing to install. <code>local</code> publishes on <code>127.0.0.1</code> only and derives <code>localhost</code>: nothing is looked up anywhere and nothing off the machine can connect.</p>
  <p>It is one setting and not two knobs because either half alone is a half-measure: a <code>.localhost</code> name with the proxy on <code>0.0.0.0</code> leaves the port open to anyone sending the right <code>Host</code> header, and a loopback bind with public hostnames still puts your naming in a stranger's DNS logs. <code>doctor</code> reports where the proxy actually listens and warns when that is not what the multiverse asks for.</p>
  <h3 id="expose-risk">What <code>lan</code> actually exposes</h3>
  <p>Worth reading once before choosing it. A development stack usually runs with debug pages on, seeded data and nothing in front of its mail catcher, so anyone on the network can read every message a universe sends, password resets included. Fine on a home network; not in a café, hotel or shared office.</p>
  <p>Those hostnames are public DNS names pointing at a private address, so a web page you happen to open can make your browser send requests to a universe. Reading the answer needs permissive CORS, which development stacks often have. Under <code>localhost</code> the browser blocks that outright.</p>
</section>

<section id="ports">
  <h2 id="ports"><a href="#ports">Why nothing publishes a port</a></h2>
  <p>Separate multiverse names, networks and volumes count for nothing while two universes map the same host port. So published ports are dropped — the <code>ports: !reset []</code> above — and a universe is reached through the proxy. A service that genuinely needs its port back asks for it, in the manifest's <code>publish:</code> list or with a label:</p>
  <Terminal kind="file"><pre><span class="k">services</span>:
  <span class="k">db</span>:
    <span class="k">labels</span>:
      <span class="k">dev.multiversectl.publish</span>: <span class="s">"true"</span></pre></Terminal>
  <p>Only one universe can hold such a port at a time, which is the trade. <code>ls -o wide</code> has a <code>PUBLISHED</code> column, so you can see that the answer is <code>none</code> everywhere it should be.</p>
</section>

<section id="inside">
  <h2 id="inside"><a href="#inside">Talking between services</a></h2>
  <p>Inside one app, services reach each other by compose service name on the app's own network — <code>db</code>, <code>redis</code>, <code>mailpit</code> — exactly as they would without multiversectl.</p>
  <p>Between apps of a universe, use its hostnames through the proxy. One asymmetry under <code>expose: local</code>: a <code>.localhost</code> name resolves to the calling container's own loopback, not to the proxy, so container-to-container calls by hostname do not work. For both privacy and those calls, keep a resolvable domain and bind to loopback: <code>domain: 127-0-0-1.traefik.me</code> with <code>expose: local</code> is a name that only ever answers <code>127.0.0.1</code>.</p>
</section>

<section id="shared">
  <h2 id="shared"><a href="#shared">Running several multiverses at once</a></h2>
  <p>The proxy belongs to the machine and serves every multiverse. It listens on the <strong>union</strong> of what the multiverses with universes running ask for, each port published the way that multiverse's <code>expose</code> says: a <code>local</code> multiverse's entrypoints stay on <code>127.0.0.1</code>, a <code>lan</code> one's on every interface. Starting a universe adds its multiverse's ports and moves nothing that another multiverse's universes use. With one multiverse nothing changes: the same ports, the same URLs.</p>
  <p>So two multiverses coexist as long as they do not want the <em>same port</em> published two ways. Give them entrypoint ports of their own:</p>
  <Terminal kind="file"><pre><span class="c"># alpha's multiverse.yaml</span>
<span class="k">spec</span>:
  <span class="k">expose</span>: local
  <span class="k">proxy</span>:
    <span class="k">entrypoints</span>:
      <span class="k">web</span>: 80

<span class="c"># beta's multiverse.yaml</span>
<span class="k">spec</span>:
  <span class="k">expose</span>: lan
  <span class="k">proxy</span>:
    <span class="k">entrypoints</span>:
      <span class="k">web</span>: 8080</pre></Terminal>
  <p>Their names may be the same: the proxy tells the ports apart, and routes use the proxy's name for the port. When the last universe of a multiverse stops, the ports only it needed are closed, so a port opened to the network does not outlive the universe that asked for it. With nothing running at all the proxy is left as it is.</p>
  <p>When they do want the same port, the clash is reported before anything is built, by <code>universe new</code>, <code>universe up</code>, <code>system proxy status</code> and <code>doctor</code>, naming both sides:</p>
  <Terminal kind="output"><pre><span class="err">error: universe 3 of beta cannot be served by the shared proxy together with the universes that run now: nothing was changed.</span>
  - universe 3 of beta (expose lan) and universes 1, 2 of alpha (expose local) both want port 80, on
    0.0.0.0 and on 127.0.0.1, and a port can only be published one way; give one of beta and alpha other
    proxy entrypoint ports (spec.proxy.entrypoints), give them the same `expose`, or stop the universes of
    one of them first</pre></Terminal>
  <p class="muted">Different TLS settings on one port clash the same way. Certificates never do: the proxy presents all of them. Different proxy networks still cannot share the one proxy; <code>doctor</code> says so.</p>
</section>
</Page>
